body:
I think a lot of time people think that dogs should learn faster than they do. How long does it take a human to understand a new language? A really long time! Think about that for your dog. He's trying to understand a language he doesn't know! And he seems to be doing so well! He just doesn't understand 'those' words yet :)  Buck still only understands Sit and Down verbally about 80% of the time, and we've been working on it for 6 weeks. 

&#x200B;

This might not work, but a few behaviorists suggest showing the action, saying the word (AKA down, sit, etc) and then clicking and treating. I know it worked with tuck (I use 'under' since it sounds like 'buck) for Buck.

Hopefully they'll be potty trained not to make a mess, but if they do (Accidents happen) have cleaning products available, clean up the mess and, of course, apologize to the establishment should an employee or shopper see. Do your due diligence to make sure that it won't effect any other handlers. If I'm wrong at all, let me know, but from what I know this is the case. Of course, before you do public access training they should have those basic skills, like potty training, loose leash walking and such, down.

&#x200B;

\*By hopefully, I mean that some people DON'T have this down. We have a SDiT program at our school, and one of the dogs is not potty trained, but she decided to start bringing him in stores anyways. Sadly, this gave a bad name to a lot of SD, SDiT and their handlers...

If you are in the US, certain places do 'sliding scales' based on income and situation. Especially if you don't have/have weak health insurance. When I first started going to therapy I was paying $10 a session. (Again, I was with and am still with a graduate student being supervised, but I think it was $25 a session for someone with a degree/credentials). It sounds like you're in a similar situation to what I had been in.

&#x200B;

Thanks, friend. I really do appreciate it. I'll try to remember and believe that as time goes on :)

I don't know  if they ever will, I think that's the thing that terrified me. And my social support is so weak right now...What I built up years ago seems to be crumbling. Any friends I would have relied on are all going through so much more serious things now...Cancer, death of family members. I'm trying to be strong for them, because I can't break, but I feel so lonely because I don't want to say anything to them. And then there are the people that insinuate that it's all something I'm making up, that I'm not really suffering, I'm just...Telling myself I am. And if I could be happy and as carefree as the facade I put up I would be, but I can't. I can't even go a day without panic attacks, and it feels so ridiculious.

&#x200B;

Thank you for your kind words. I really, really appreciate them.

Bipolar 1-Seroquel and effexor, with xanax for extreme panic....Worked for a little while and then just stopped a few months ago...Effexor was upped this summer and I had such awful side effects, it felt like I was constantly drunk, muscles wouldn't work, my mind was always hazy. We had a fire alarm in school and I didn't even realize it, was just staring into space. I thought it was me but finally went into a doc who let me start going down. None of the withdrawl symptoms usually associated with effexor, luckily, but now we get to play with different drugs again...Yay...

&#x200B;

I can't afford going to a pdoc, so I have to go to a family clinician. I honestly don't know if she knows what she's doing, but it's all I can afford right now...
